# Coda terminal integration

> For full reference, see [`docs/developer/CODA.md`](../../docs/developer/CODA.md). The API contract
> itself is owned by the separate `grafana-coda-app` plugin — see that repo's `docs/API.md`.

## Ownership boundary

**Pathfinder owns the terminal UI. It does not own the backend.** VM provisioning, SSH, the relay
handshake, quota, and the Coda credentials all live in the **`grafana-coda-app`** plugin. Pathfinder
is its first API consumer.

```
Pathfinder frontend (React / xterm.js)
    │  POST /api/plugins/grafana-coda-app/resources/v1/sessions   → { sessionId, channel }
    │  Grafana Live subscribe + publish on the returned channel
    ↓
grafana-coda-app backend (Go)
    ├─ REST ──→ Coda server  (VM CRUD, catalogues, auth)
    └─ WSS  ──→ Relay ──→ SSH ──→ EC2 VM
```

There is **no Go code in this repo for Coda**. `pkg/` is an App Platform read proxy only. If a task
mentions `CodaClient`, `RunStream`, `ConnectSSHViaRelay`, or `WSConn`, that work belongs in the
`grafana-coda-app` repo, not here.

## The session handshake

VM resolution takes up to ~3 minutes, so the REST call only reserves intent and returns immediately;
provisioning happens after the client subscribes, where status frames can drive the progress bar.

1. `createSession(vmOpts)` → `POST /v1/sessions` with `{ template?, app?, scenario? }`.
2. The response carries `sessionId` and a fully-formed `channel` string.
3. `sessionChannelAddress(channel)` splits it into a `LiveChannelAddress`; the path is **opaque** —
   never construct or parse it by hand.
4. Subscribe. The backend provisions, connects SSH, and streams.
5. Publish `{ type: 'input' | 'resize' }` back over the same channel with `{ useSocket: true }`.

A scenario ID containing slashes (e.g. `otel-examples/cost-control`) travels in the JSON body. The
old channel-path grammar had to spread it across trailing segments; do not reintroduce that.

## Live frame shape

One shape only. `parseTerminalOutput` unwraps a single string field holding JSON:

```
frame name: "coda.session.v1"
fields:     [ { name: "event", type: string } ]
```

`SessionEvent.type` is one of `output`, `error`, `connected`, `disconnected`, `status`, `heartbeat`. An
`error` frame also carries a `code` from the same closed set as the REST errors — branch on that, and
fall back to the `error` sentence for a code this build does not recognise or a backend that sends
none. New codes are additive within v1; an unknown one is never fatal.

## Exec is session-scoped

`coda-exit-zero:<command>` and the challenge block's setup both call
`execInSession(sessionId, { command, readyFile, timeoutMs })`. There is no "current session"
fallback — a missing session id is a "not ready" state, not an error to surface raw.

Knowing when a session has died is the **consumer's** job now: `cleanup()` in
`useTerminalLive.hook.ts` clears `sessionId`, and every terminating path that does not call it clears
the id itself. `getTerminalSessionId()` additionally returns `null` unless the status is `connected`.
Do not reintroduce a retained id — exec reuses the stream's SSH client, so it can only be spent on a
session the backend has forgotten.

`readyFile` gates the command behind that file existing. That is a **UI-race guard, not a security
boundary** — the learner has full shell access to the same VM. Use `PATHFINDER_READY_FILE` from
`coda-api.ts` rather than writing the literal: the setup write and the check must agree.

Classify failures with `isNotReady` / `isRoleForbidden` / `isUnavailable`, never by matching on the
message. Several distinct failures share a status.

## gcx credentials

`terminal-connect` with `gcx: true` installs a Grafana credential into the VM after the terminal
connects, so the `gcx` CLI in every sandbox image can talk to this Grafana as the learner.
`provisionGcx(sessionId, { token? })` in `coda-api.ts` wraps the SDK call, because this module owns the
only `CodaClient` instance.

**Minting happens in the browser and cannot move into the plugin.** Grafana refuses to create a service
account whose role exceeds the caller's own, and a plugin's managed service account has no basic role,
so the backend can mint nothing usable — by design, not an oversight. Do not "simplify" this by asking
the Coda backend to mint.

**Never ship a mint-only UI.** `serviceaccounts:create` is Admin by default while sessions are open to
Editors, so most people who can open a terminal cannot mint. `mint_forbidden` is a **branch** — reveal a
token field — not an error to surface raw. Use `isMintForbidden`, never a status or message match.

**Do not complete the step on connection alone.** With `gcx` set, the step is done when the credential is
in; ticking it off at `connected` marks success while every command it exists to enable would still fail
unauthenticated. `executeStep` must likewise return `false` while the credential is outstanding, or Do
Section forges the completion.

**A `404` on the credential route means an old plugin, not a bad session.** There is no capability flag
for it — `features` is still `["exec.readyFile"]` — and the call only ever follows a session we just
connected, so `session_not_found` there is read as "Coda plugin predates 1.3.0". Do not do version
arithmetic on `pluginVersion` instead.

**A refusal must not dead-end a guide.** The terminal is connected either way, so offer "Continue
without gcx" and let `is-terminal-active` steps proceed.

## Availability gating

The terminal is optional, so `grafana-coda-app` is **not** a declared plugin dependency. Availability
is resolved at runtime:

| Gate | Where |
|------|-------|
| `jsonData.enableCodaTerminal` | Pathfinder's own setting (default `false`) |
| `useCodaPluginAvailable()` | `isAppPluginInstalled('grafana-coda-app')` from boot data, then `isAppPluginEnabled` — cached per page load |
| `isDevMode` | terminal panel only |

`docs-panel.tsx` needs all three; `BlockPalette.tsx` needs the first two. `useCodaTerminalGate()`
bundles the first two as one verdict (`checking` / `disabled` / `plugin-missing` / `configured`) for
callers that need to *say* which one is unmet. Never assume the plugin is present — every failure path
must degrade quietly.

**The first gate is enforced before the probe, not just around its consumer.** `enableCodaTerminal`
is off by default, so an installation that never turned the terminal on asks nothing about Coda at all.
`useCodaPluginAvailability(shouldProbe)` and `useCodaSessionEligibility(shouldLoad)` take the caller's
own gate — both parameters are **required**, so a new call site cannot reintroduce an ungated probe by
omission — and `useCodaTerminalGate()` feeds `enabled` into the first. `useCodaOptions` reads the gate
itself, covering every block-editor form. A probe that fires anyway is the bug: it 404s on every page
load of every stack without Coda.

**Absence is not a fault, and must not be reported as one.** `isAppPluginEnabled` fetches the plugin's
*settings*, and core logs that 404 twice through `console.error` and pushes it into its own error
tracking. `isAppPluginInstalled` answers the same question without ever touching
`/api/plugins/grafana-coda-app/*` — from boot data, or from core's own plugin LIST under
`pluginsUseMTPlugins` — so it runs first and only a definite "not installed" short-circuits. Missing
helper, rejection, or `true` all fall through, since it does not itself check *enabled*.

**The terminal needs Grafana 13.1**, and only because of that second gate: `isAppPluginEnabled` is
served by core's own `@grafana/runtime` and does not exist before 13.1, where it throws synchronously
rather than rejecting. Nothing else in the path needs it — the SDK uses only `getBackendSrv().fetch`
and `getGrafanaLiveSrv` — so the sandbox would otherwise work on 12.3–13.0. This floor is deliberately
**not** in `plugin.json`: `grafanaDependency` stays `>=12.3.0-0` because raising it would block the
whole plugin from installing for an optional, dev-mode-gated feature. `isCodaProbeSupported()` is the
one place that distinguishes "cannot ask" from "plugin absent"; anything that explains unavailability
to an operator must call it, or it reports a missing plugin on a stack where the plugin is fine.

Two questions the backend answers on `GET /v1/capabilities`, both absent on a Coda plugin older than
1.2.0, and absent must always mean "no evidence of a problem":

- **Is Coda usable?** `isCodaUsable(capabilities)`, not `registered` — which stays true for a refresh
  token that expired 90 days ago while every call 401s. `CodaBackendStatus` reads it, and surfaces
  `configErrors` because those name what stops registration.
- **May *this* user start one?** `useCodaSessionEligibility()` → `checking` / `eligible` /
  `role_forbidden` / `unknown`. Four states, not a boolean: on `unknown` and `checking`, attempt the
  call and keep handling `403 role_forbidden`. Use `caller.minimumSessionRole` for the message only —
  never rank the user's role against it, since the plugin context carries only the basic role and RBAC
  cannot grant past it.

**`useTerminalContext()` returning non-null does not mean there is a terminal.** `TerminalProvider`
mounts unconditionally; `TerminalPanel` registers the real `connect` only behind the three gates, so
an unregistered context has `connect === null` and `openTerminal` silently no-ops. Anything that waits
on a connection must check `isTerminalRegistered` or it hangs (issue #1541).

## Key files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `src/integrations/coda/coda-api.ts` | The only module that knows the Coda plugin id. `createSession`, `execInSession`, `getCapabilities`, `provisionGcx`, `sessionChannelAddress` |
| `src/integrations/coda/useTerminalLive.hook.ts` | Live subscription, publish, animated provision progress bar, 35 s handshake timeout |
| `src/integrations/coda/TerminalContext.tsx` | Shared context (`isTerminalRegistered`, `error`) + module-level `getTerminalConnectionStatus()` / `getTerminalSessionId()` for the requirements checker |
| `src/integrations/coda/TerminalPanel.tsx` | xterm.js panel: fit/resize, scrollback persistence, WebGL renderer, auto-reconnect |
| `src/integrations/coda/useCodaAvailability.hook.ts` | Runtime plugin detection and `useCodaTerminalGate()` |
| `src/integrations/coda/terminal-storage.ts` | localStorage/sessionStorage keys for panel state, scrollback, last VM opts |
| `src/requirements-manager/checks/coda.ts` | `coda-exit-zero:` check (always gated on `PATHFINDER_READY_FILE`) |
| `src/components/AppConfig/CodaBackendStatus.tsx` | Reports backend availability; links to the Coda plugin's own config page |
| `src/components/interactive-tutorial/challenge-block.tsx` | CTF-style block: setup script, sentinel write, check-my-work |

## Configuration

Pathfinder keeps exactly one Coda setting:

| Key | Default | Description |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `enableCodaTerminal` | `false` | Whether Pathfinder shows terminal UI at all |

Everything else — API URL, relay URL, enrollment key, refresh token, registration state — belongs to
`grafana-coda-app` and is configured at `/plugins/grafana-coda-app`. **Secure settings cannot be
migrated across plugins**, so operators must re-enter their enrollment key there.

## Guide block types

`terminal`, `terminal-connect`, and `challenge` are unchanged in the guide schema:

```json
{
  "type": "terminal-connect",
  "content": "Connect to an nginx sandbox:",
  "buttonText": "Connect to nginx sandbox",
  "vmTemplate": "vm-aws-sample-app",
  "vmApp": "nginx"
}
```

| Field | Default | Description |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `vmTemplate` | `""` (→ `vm-aws`) | VM template to provision |
| `vmApp` | `""` | App name for `vm-aws-sample-app` |
| `vmScenario` | `""` | Scenario ID for `vm-aws-alloy-scenario` (may contain `/`) |
| `gcx` | `false` | Also install a gcx credential — see above |

## Constraints

- Credentials (private key, VM IP) are never exposed to the frontend — only the Coda plugin's
  backend touches SSH.
- Never hardcode `grafana-coda-app` outside `coda-api.ts`.
- Never construct a Live channel path by hand; use the `channel` the backend returned.
- The requirements checker runs outside React — reach session state through the module-level getters,
  not the context.
